<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Raj of Sarawak</h1><p class="st-meta">1841 CE – 1946 CE · capital Kuching</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Kingdom in northern Borneo ruled for a century by an English family, the White Rajahs of the Brooke dynasty.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The English adventurer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-brooke">James Brooke</a> helped the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate</a> put down a revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidayuh">Bidayuh</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuching">Kuching</a> in 1839 to 1841. As a reward the sultan made him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-of-sarawak">Rajah of Sarawak</a> in 1841. Brooke and his successors then took further river basins from Brunei until Sarawak far exceeded the sultanate in size.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Brookes ruled personally, with a small European staff and Malay chiefs, and governed through longhouse headmen among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iban-people">Iban</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayak-people">Dayak peoples</a>. They suppressed piracy and headhunting, often with Iban levies, and limited European plantation companies. Chinese settlers grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and mined gold. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-brooke-rajah-of-sarawak">Charles Brooke</a> ruled for nearly fifty years, and Sarawak became a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1888.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Japanese occupation from 1941 to 1945, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vyner-brooke">Charles Vyner Brooke</a> ceded Sarawak to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> in 1946 despite strong local opposition, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/crown-colony-of-sarawak">Crown Colony of Sarawak</a>. It joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia</a> in 1963.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/raj-of-sarawak.jpg" alt="Raj of Sarawak" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Defunct, extinct historical state, sucessor state: Sarawak · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sarawak_(1870%E2%80%931946,_1963%E2%80%931973).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1841 CE – 1946 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kuching</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>James Brooke, Charles Brooke, Charles Vyner Brooke</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion, Sunni Islam, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay, Iban, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Sarawak, Brooke Raj, Sarawak</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1894&amp;focus=raj-of-sarawak" aria-label="Raj of Sarawak on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7745 2119 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Raj of Sarawak lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8065 2375L8178 2269M8065 2375L4997 651M8065 2375L8065 2375M8065 2375L8065 2375"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8178 2269h0M4997 651h0M8065 2375h0M8065 2375h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8065 2375h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8065 2375h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kuching. <a href="/atlas?year=1894&amp;focus=raj-of-sarawak">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
